---
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro"
import ArticleLayout from "../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro"
import { PAGES } from "../lib/pages"
import { DAEMON_COMMANDS, SITE } from "../lib/site"

const VAULT_COMMANDS = `npx bookmarks-but-better vault add reading ~/vaults/reading
npx bookmarks-but-better vault add archive ~/vaults/archive`
---

<ArticleLayout
  title={PAGES.daemon.title}
  description={PAGES.daemon.description}
  eyebrow="Markdown vaults"
  heading="Your bookmarks as Markdown files you own"
  intro="A small, optional background process serves your bookmarks to the extension from a folder of Markdown files. It listens on 127.0.0.1 only, needs no account, and is how Bookmarks But Better works in Safari."
>
  <h2>Why keep bookmarks as Markdown?</h2>
  <p>
    Browsers keep bookmarks in an internal database that is hard to read, back
    up or edit with your own tools. A vault turns that around: your bookmarks
    are plain Markdown files in a folder you choose, with no hidden database, so
    the folder stays usable in a text editor, in Obsidian and in Git.
  </p>
  <p>
    The daemon serves the vault to the extension over <code>127.0.0.1</code>.
    It makes no outbound requests of its own, and nothing is uploaded anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <div class="not-prose space-y-4">
    {
      DAEMON_COMMANDS.map((command) => (
        <CodeBlock label={command.label} code={command.code} />
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <p>
    All three install the same release, verified against its published SHA-256
    checksum, into a user-local directory with no <code>sudo</code>, and start
    the background service. <code>npx bookmarks-but-better</code> asks where your
    vault should live, and afterwards it is where you check the status, add or remove
    a vault, update or uninstall. The scripts take the vault on the command line
    and never ask. Step-by-step instructions are in the{" "}
    <a href={SITE.daemonDocs}>install guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Connect the extension</h2>
  <p>
    In the extension, open <strong>Settings → Sources</strong> and connect to the
    daemon's address, <code>127.0.0.1:52222</code> by default. The extension asks
    for permission to reach localhost at that moment, never at install time, and
    reports an unreachable daemon as an error rather than silently falling back.
    See <a href="/docs/daemon/connect/">Connect the extension</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Several vaults, one daemon</h2>
  <div class="not-prose">
    <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code={VAULT_COMMANDS} />
  </div>
  <p>
    Each vault has its own directory and appears in the extension as its own
    source, with its own tree and search. Sources are never merged, and every
    operation affects only the active source. See <a
      href="/docs/daemon/multiple-vaults/">Multiple vaults</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Backups</h2>
  <p>
    A vault is just files, so the tools you already use back it up: a copy, Time
    Machine, rsync or Git. <a href="/docs/daemon/backups/"
      >Backing up your vault</a
    > covers what to copy and how to restore.
  </p>

  <h2>Safari</h2>
  <p>
    Safari gives extensions no access to its bookmarks and has no new-tab
    override. There, the extension works only with a vault: connect the daemon,
    then open the dashboard from the toolbar popup. See <a
      href="/docs/start/safari/">Safari</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Uninstall</h2>
  <p>
    <code>npx bookmarks-but-better uninstall</code> stops and removes the background
    service and the daemon. Your vault is a directory of Markdown files and stays
    exactly where you put it. See <a href="/docs/daemon/uninstall/">Uninstall</a
    > and <a href="/docs/daemon/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
